{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "comment": "X-02 \u00a72, \u00a76.1-\u00a76.3 (rev 14). 'fetch': tls.fetch's tagged result, {ok:{mode:https,cert,key,origin,hsts}} | {ok:{mode:http_loopback,origin,hsts}} | {unavailable:ListenFailure}; 'expect' is the decoded form or 'refused'. https needs a non-empty cert and key; http_loopback refuses a cert or key of any value, null included. Accepted rows serialize back to the same JSON. 'listen_failure' is ListenFailure alone (status, doctor and unavailable): {reason:origin_invalid} | {reason:hsts_invalid} | {reason:tls_name_mismatch,uncovered:[canonical origins]}. 'refusals': the tls.set/bootstrap refusals with their wire forms and whether each op journals them (only tls.set's tls_key_mismatch; no bootstrap refusal is ever stored). After the journal lookup, tls.set and bootstrap.tls check in this order: origin_invalid, hsts_invalid, tls_invalid, tls_key_mismatch, tls_not_valid, tls_name_mismatch. 'bad_refusals' don't decode. 'listen_config': the invalid-configuration rule on a stored listen configuration; 'result' is 'valid', 'origin_invalid' (with the OriginError code, or with 'field' when a listener field, address, port or scheme, is missing or malformed: origin can't be checked against it) or 'hsts_invalid', which the host reports as that ListenFailure and bootstrap and tls.set refuse with as that code. A missing origin or hsts has no default. 'bootstrap': bootstrap's result, a HostOpResult that is never 'accepted' ({status:accepted} and any other status don't decode): completed {result:{session}} or rejected {refusal}, the refusal a TlsRefusal or {code:tls_required} / {code:tls_mismatch} / {code:already_bootstrapped} / {code:bootstrap_finished}; 'expect' is 'completed', the refusal's code, or 'refused'. No bootstrap refusal is ever stored. The host decides in this order, the journal lookup first as for every journaled op: on a non-empty registry, an operation_id other than the recorded bootstrap's (or a registry setup has no record of) is already_bootstrapped whatever else holds; the recorded one is bootstrap_finished once bootstrap has finished, and otherwise replays (sign-in, then fingerprint, then pair: another pair is tls_mismatch); on an empty registry, origin_invalid, hsts_invalid, tls_required, then tls.set's order (tls_invalid, tls_key_mismatch, tls_not_valid, tls_name_mismatch). 'timestamps': tls_not_valid's not_before and not_after, RFC 3339 in one profile, YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ (UTC, uppercase T and Z, no fractional seconds, no offset, a real date and time); 'valid' says whether it decodes. A refused one makes the whole refusal fail to decode.",
